WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.410
Our school wanted to move the

2
00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:03.610
start of the day later.

3
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.350
Some said students would sleep

4
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.970
more and learn better.

5
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.730
Others worried about parents' work.

6
00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.270
How could data help decide?

7
00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.830
Today: how a school can use data to decide

8
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:20.430
well, and when data is not enough.

9
00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.390
Data cannot make a decision for you.

10
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.470
It shows what is likely to

11
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.810
happen and to whom.

12
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.980
People still weigh what matters

13
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.060
most, and they own the decision.

14
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.780
We wrote one clear question: if we

15
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.920
start thirty minutes later, will

16
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.480
lateness and tiredness fall?

17
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.820
Then we chose what to measure before

18
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.520
collecting anything, so we could not pick

19
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.240
the numbers we liked afterwards.

20
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.040
Two classes started later for six weeks

21
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.560
while two similar classes did not.

22
00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.360
Late arrivals per week fell from

23
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.820
twelve to five in the trial classes,

24
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.940
and barely changed in the others.

25
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.340
Numbers were only half the story.

26
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.680
We also asked parents and

27
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.940
pupils, and some families could

28
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.340
not manage the later finish.

29
00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.150
Their voices changed our plan

30
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.790
more than any chart.

31
00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.710
Ms Farah shared the results with

32
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.450
everyone, including what did not work.

33
00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.670
Now any student can bring a question to

34
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.410
the school's monthly data meeting.

35
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.770
Small honest trials have become

36
00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:30.910
how the school decides.

37
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:32.960
Quick check!

38
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.350
Why did we keep two similar classes on

39
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.610
the old start time during the trial?

40
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.050
To compare fairly.

41
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.940
Lateness might have fallen anyway,

42
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.080
because of the weather or the season.

43
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.800
The other classes show what would

44
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.360
have happened without the change.

45
00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.050
In the app, open the decision lab.

46
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.150
Change how much each thing matters for a

47
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.390
class trip, and watch the best place

48
00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.570
change while the scores stay the same.

49
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:03.340
Your turn.

50
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.770
The data shows a later

51
00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.490
start cuts lateness.

52
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.900
Who should make the final decision?

53
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.620
The school's people, using the

54
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.600
data and the voices together.

55
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.160
Remember: ask one clear question, run

56
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.060
a fair trial, listen as well as

57
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.480
count, and share what you find.

58
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.420
Try it yourself: think of one change

59
00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.520
